<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!-- Deliberately empty, and deliberately not the core's entitlements.plist.

     The app claims no hardened-runtime exception because it needs none: it
     embeds no interpreter, and is not sandboxed, so its network access and the
     directories the user picks need nothing declared.  The core it spawns is a
     separate process signed separately, and the three exceptions CPython needs
     are on that binary's own signature, where they apply.

     It does load code that is not Apple's — the embedded Sparkle framework and
     the helpers it launches — and still needs nothing here.  Library
     validation is satisfied by a matching Team ID, which is what signing the
     nested code with the app's own identity buys; a disable-library-validation
     entitlement is what a bundle reaches for when its nested code was signed
     by somebody else.

     An empty claim stated in a file of its own is what keeps the two apart.
     Reusing entitlements.plist here would hand a Swift app the right to map
     unsigned executable memory and to load unvalidated libraries, for no
     reason beyond the file already existing. -->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict/>
</plist>
